<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russo-Persian Treaty of Friendship (1921)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Soviet–Persian Treaty of 1921</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russo-Persian Treaty of Friendship was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 26 February 1921 between Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Russia renounced tsarist concessions, debts and privileges and agreed to withdraw its forces from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, ending its support for the Soviet republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>. A clause allowed Soviet troops to enter Iran if a third power threatened Russia from Iranian territory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russo-persian-treaty-of-friendship-1921.jpg" alt="Russo-Persian Treaty of Friendship (1921)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Russo-Persian_Treaty_of_Friendship_(1921).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty's original purpose was to keep White Russian counter-revolutionary forces, who had fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> after the Bolshevik Revolution, from attacking the Soviets from Iranian territory, as had happened as late as 1918. It was signed for Iran by ʿAli-Qoli Masʿud Ansari Moshaver al-Mamalek, and for the Russian SFSR by Georgy Chicherin and his deputy Lev Karakhan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>All earlier agreements between the two states, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> among them, were cancelled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> received full and equal shipping rights in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, with the right to fly their national flags on their commercial vessels.</p><p>Article 5 bound each party to prohibit on its territory any organisation or armed force whose aim was hostility to the other, to stop a hostile third party from importing or carrying across its land material that could be used against the other, and to prevent the presence of a third party's forces that would threaten the other's frontiers or safety. Article 6 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the right to advance its troops into the Persian interior if a third power used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> as a base against Russia and the Persian government could not end the threat once called upon to do so; Russia undertook to withdraw them as soon as the danger had passed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Articles 5 and 6 were debated vigorously in the Persian parliament, and the Shah asked the Soviet side to clarify them. In a letter, the Soviet diplomatic representative Rothstein replied that they applied only to preparations for a considerable armed attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> or its allied republics by partisans of the overthrown regime or by foreign powers supporting them, and not to verbal or written attacks by Persian groups or Russian émigrés. Anti-British sentiment led the parliament to accept the treaty without alteration.</p><p>Ratifications were exchanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> on 26 February 1922, and the treaty was registered in the League of Nations Treaty Series on 7 June 1922. It later served as the basis for a Soviet occupation in 1941, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> repudiated articles 5 and 6 in 1979.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Russo-Persian Treaty of Friendship” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Soviet-Persian Treaty</b> of February 1921, Soviet Russia withdrew its forces.</blockquote><a href="/state/persian-socialist-soviet-republic">Persian Socialist Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
